s for math, less so for literacy The data gives a general picture about student performance on the CMAS, tests in several subjects \u2018By Jason Gonzales | May 30 FROM CHALKBEAT DETROIT Detroit\u2019s Paul Robeson Malcolm X school gets new leadership as longtime principal is reassigned The K8 school, Paul Robeson Malcolm X, has already been assigned a new principal By Micah Walker, BridgeDetoit | May 29 These Colorado counties \u2014 1 red and 1 blue \u2014 offer property tax rebates to childcare programs By Ann Schimbe | May 30 \u2018Teachers, families, administrators: Where can NYC find savings in the $45 billion schools budget? trudge looks like tothe people on the ground and wiere you see the biggest \u2018By Michael Elsen-Rooney and Alex Zimmerman | May 29 FROM CHALKBEAT NATIONAL Has the Trump administration returned education to the states? Dig in at a Chalkbeat virtual event. Rhode Island Commissioner of Education Ang\u00e9lica Infante-Green and Indiana Secretary of Education Katie Jenner. \u2018By Caroline Bauman | May29 FROM CHALKBEAT CHICAGO A divided Chicago school board postpones charter renewals until after the end of the school year After an intense discussion about how Chicago Public Schools oversees charters, the divided board moved to table votes on four- to seven-year renewals for charter schools and networks. \u2018By Mila Koumpilova | May29 FROM CHALKBEAT NEW YORK Diving into the teacher diversity gap: Why classroom representation matters This episode of, Weekly explores how the representation gapin NYC's teaching workforce affect the classroom and looks at efforts to change the pipeline By Chalkbes Staff] May29 A tax increase on the November ballot could have a lasting impact on schools. What will IPEC decide to do? \u2018The Indianapolis Public Education Corporation must decide whether or not toputatax increase to voters in November By Slaby | May29 Philadelphia\u2019s school board approves $225 million in, budget cuts \u2018The district budget includes some $225 millon in cuts that will mean schools will ose climate staf, teachers, counselors, and substitutes. By Carly iin | May29 FROM CHALKBEAT NATIONAL \u2018The push for Al-era critical thinking risks overlooking what students need most Cognitive scientists say problem-solving and critical thinking depend on deep subject knowledge not just generic strategies. \u2018By Mate Barnum | May29 FROM CHALKBEAT NEWARK KIPP middle school students learn money management skills by creating their own businesses learn money management skills through the teacher-developed Financially LiTcourse, By Jessie Gimex | May 29 List: These partnerships allow Colorado high school graduates to get into college without applying \u2018The Colorado School of Mines, the University of Colorado Denver, and [Adams State University are among the colleges that have direct admissions agreements By Melanie Asmar | May23 FROM CHALKBEAT COLORADO How a decade-old youth apprenticeship program fell short of its goal by thousands of students CCareerWise Colorado has employed far fewer students in youth apprenticeships than leaders once envisioned. The program\u2019s challenges offer valuable le By Jaton Gonzales | May 28 ssons as bipartisan enthusiasm for on-the-job-training grows nationwide, FROM CHALKBEAT NEW YORK NYC Chancellor Kamar Samuels pledges stronger AI guardrails: \u2018Most invasive technology that we've seen\u2019 \u2018Samuels\u2019 comments mark a shiftin tone and indicate thatthe nation's largest school system is grappling with the AI backlash brewing nationwide. By Michael Elsen-Rooney | May 28 FROM CHALKBEAT CHICAGO 51 people are running in Chicago\u2019s 2026 school board election. Some may not make it to the ballot. Dozens of people filed paperwork to appear on the Nov. 3 ballot for Chicago's school board election by the required deadline. \u2018By Reema Amin | May 27 Saying students are \u2018drowning\u2019 because of technology, AFT president pushes stricter school AI limits Ina speech on education technology, andi Weingarten proposed banning student-facing Al, limiting screens in elementary classrooms, and creating Al safety standards across education. By ily Aavena | May27 PROM CHALKREAT INDIANA New to Indiana, Cambridge-backed courses are expanding in the state\u2019s high schools Here's how students could use the courses toe advanced diplomas. By Aleksandra Appleton | May27 FROM CHALKBEAT TENNESSEE Memphis school board passes $1.7 billion budget on heels of state takeover Become a Chalkbeat sponsor 4 million+ students | 350 million reading sessions More on the latest K-12 digital data @& Sora Follow Chalkbeat G GOOGLE PREFERRED SOURCE Stories you may have missed 57 Philadelphia schools will go remote Wednesday due to heat NYC preschool offers flat despite Mamdani push to expand enrollment \u2018Why the Philadelphia school district will phase out a special education Program Report: Michigan\u2019s neediest students lack access to advanced coursework. More than 19,000 Colorado childcare workers benefit from new state tax credit in first year Become a Chalkbeat sponsor SUMMIT owes Ww ooo + 9 Ifyou value Chalkbeat, consider making a donation Chalkbeatis a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to providing the information families and educators need, but this kind of workisn't possible without your help. DONATE Connect with your community Find upcoming events EVENTS Events June - \u201cCongressional Briefing: Building an Evidence \u2018Connected Learning\u201d we for Career \u2018June I - Viral Chalkbeat event: Has she Trump administration \u201creturned \u2018education to the states\u201d? June 17 - Apprenticing Students to Read and Writein the Disciplines July 21 - 2026 NK360\u00b0 Summer \u2018Webinar Series | American Revolution: Haudenosaunee Perseverance Become a Chalkbeat sponsor CONFERENCE Unlock the power of financial education June 23, 2026 | 12\u201d\u2122-4\"\u2122 ET", "ocr_text_len": 7907}
