 our publication. For more details, refer to our Privacy Policy) and Cookie Policy. Google Analytics Collect anonymous website tafe data to determine which pages: {and topics are most popular with our readers. Pressure mounts on the Iran war ceasefire; Taiwan and China face off at a disputed atoll in the South China Sea; a supercharged El Nifio threatens to compound an already dire food security outlook; and the House GOP defies President Trump on the Ukraine war. DP World's Brazil-Africa Corridor: Rise of a New South Atlantic Trade Lane? SITUATION REPORTS - Jun 08, 2026 Geopolitical Orientation on the Ballot in Armenia Elections SITUATION REPORTS - Jun 05, 2026 DP World's vision of a Brazil-Africa Corridor signals the steady extension of Gulf logistics control into the South Atlantic. But the project, which would alter supply chains for food, energy, and minerals, is far from geopolitcally neutral Washington Risks Repeating Israel's Strategic Mistake in the War of Attrition SITUATION REPORTS - Jun 05, 2026 Armenians head to the polls on June 7. The election outcome will affect not just domestic governance, but Armenia's geopolitical orientation going forward, with consequences for the peace process with Azerbaijan and wider stability across the South Caucasus. @ How the Ukraine War Reshaped Russia's Long-Range Strike Capabilities BACKGROUNDERS - Jun 05, 2026 The risk faced by the United States in the Strait of Hormuz mirrors Israel's War of Attrition against Egypt: What presents as a contained skirmish may actually be setting the stage for a future regional war. Washington's New Terrorist Designations Risks Derailing US- Brazil Relations SITUATION REPORTS - Jun 04, 2026 = AUS terrorist designation of two major Brazilian organized crime outfits is not a matter of law enforcement - it's a geopolitical weapon, speak nothing of a compliance nightmare for regional banks, and it will reverberate across US-Brazilian relations for years to come. Southeast Asia\u2019s Quiet Verdict on US Power OPINION - Jun 04, 2026 Russia has re-invented its long-range strike capabilities over the course of the Ukraine war, moving from import dependency and exhausted stockpiles to a domestic production capacity that is now eclipsing NATO. Uneven Playing Field: Mexico, Iran, and the Geopolitics of the World Cup OPINION - Jun 04, 2026 The World Cup has afforded space for Mexico's President Sheinbaum to play a constructive role in defusing Middle East tensions, but asymmetric realities will make it hard to repeat the trick on Cuba or the cartels, The Geopolitics of Geo- Engineering: Weather Warfare vs. Climate Security BACKGROUNDERS - Jun 03, 2026 Southeast Asia is not moving towards China. It is moving away from US dependence. Inflate Away the Debt? Strategic Logic and Risks of a Weak Dollar Regime BACKGROUNDERS - Jun 02, 2026 Geoengineering risks transforming the climate itself into an arena of geopolitical competition, where the atmosphere, sunlight, and weather systems become objects of strategic control. Geopolitical Dimensions of Forced Labor Governance SITUATION REPORTS - Jun 02, 2026 Mounting debt burdens are narrowing Washington's fiscal space and eroding confidence in the US dollar's reserve currency status. However, history shows that severe fiscal conditions can be reversed; and if not, the more likely outcome is a gradual erosion of confidence in USD assets, not an abrupt collapse. # Geopolitics Weekly (Iran War Deal, DRC Ebola Outbreak, Quad Revived) FORECASTS - Jun 01, 2026 Geopelitical Monito Grorouivics eect Examining the latest diplomatic efforts to hammer out a framework deal in the Iran war, signs that the Ebola outbreak in central Africa is worse than the data reflects, and the tentative return of the Quad as a player in the Indo-Pacific security architecture, Lessons Forgotten: Critical Minerals and Partisan Ideology in the U.S. OPINION - May 29, 2026 Global labor governance is fracturing, leaving \u2018manufacturers caught between competing legal systems that, in effect, force them to choose between maintaining access to the Chinese input networks or Western markets. A Hard Offer to Refuse: Ukraine's Strategic Pitch to a Middle East in Flux BACKGROUNDERS - Jun 01, 2026 Russia's support for its Iranian ally imperils years of economic and diplomatic engagement with Gulf states. Ukraine has taken advantage of this contradiction, offering Gulf states no-strings- attached operational expertise against a weapon that Kyiv knows too well. The resulting erosion of Russia's footprint in the Middle East will resonate for years, if not decades to come. From Crisis to Opportunity: How China Quietly Gains from the Iran War SITUATION REPORTS - May 29, 2026 Administrations from both sides of the aisle have left US critical minerals supply chains highly vulnerable amid a return to great power politics. But stockpiling initiatives like \u2018Project Vault\u2019 suggest that Washington is beginning to remember the old truism that economics and geopolitics are inseparable. The Unfinished Family Feud: Constitutional \"One China\u201d and Cross-Strait Ambiguity BACKGROUNDERS - May 29, 2026 The Iran war is opening strategic avenues for China to strengthen its international and domestic position in a disrupted world order. Uranium Industry Emerges as Strategic Bridge between India and Central Asia SITUATION REPORTS - May 28, 2026 Examining the evolution of constitutional \u201cOne China\u201d framework, which endures because it accommodates contradictions between Beijing and Taipei without forcing either side to concede ~ managing tensions while never fully resolving them. A Eurasian Pact Takes Shape Amid the Ruins of the Old Order OPINION - May 28, 2026 Deepening convergence between China and Russia is beginning to resemble a paradigm shift in global order \u2014 not yet a formal alliance, but something potentially more consequential: the gradual construction of a Eurasian strategic sphere designed to outlast American primacy. @ Bringing the Bullion Back: Geopolitics Returns to Global Gold Markets SITUATION REPORTS - May 27, 2026 The 4 billion USD Kazatomprom-India uranium contract and the SHANTI Act 2025 are recasting India's foothold in Central Asia. Geological Maps: Key to Securing Critical Minerals Supply Chains OPINION - May 27, 2026 = Geological mapping remains a dangerously neglected block in building Western critical mineral supply chains, hobbling the West's ability, to compete in crucial emerging markets. The USGS has the power to change that. @ Are Drones Solution to EU Deep Strike Capability Gap? SITUATION REPORTS - May 26, 2026 The New York Fed's vault remains the largest sovereign gold repository in the world, but recent withdrawals suggest that geopolitical considerations are increasingly tilting the balance toward domestic storage, even among longstanding US alles. EU governments are racing to develop drone warfare capabilities with the help of Ukraine. These efforts will help boost European strategic autonomy in the short term, but from a deterrence standpoint, drones are only half the answer. MORE ARTICLES pinion Situation Reports Commodities fconces Home About Contact us Terre: Join Now \u2018Login Russia's Gray Zone Warfare Campaign in Europe [BACKGROUNDERS -Feb 19,2026, @ Venezuela's Loss, Guyana's Strategic Opportunity SITUATION REPORTS -Jan 08,2026, 4 Siliguri Corridor: The Achilles\u2019 Heel of India [BACKGROUNDERS - Dec 12,2025 Thailand Shows the West Has Already Lost Southeast Asia SITUATION REPORTS - Dec 23,2025 IRAN WAR Iran War Ceasefire Frays, Taiwan-China South China Sea Standoff, El Nifio | Geopolitics Weekly FORECASTS -jun 08,2026 Washington Risks Repeating Israel's Strategic Mistake in the War of Atti SITUATION REPORTS -jun 05,2026 & Geopolitics Weekly (Iran War Deal, DRC Ebola Outbreak, Quad Revived) FORECASTS-jun 01,2026 CRITICAL MINERALS Lessons Forgotten: Critical Minerals and, Partisan Ideology in the U.S. \u2018OPINION - May 28, 2026 