{"_key": "monthlyreview", "source_key": "monthlyreview", "filename": "Monthly_Review.png", "image_file": "Monthly_Review.png", "ok": true, "feature_schema": "nollm_raw12_v3_lx9_v8", "nollm_features": {"s004_sensational_verbs": 1.1838, "s008_neutral_headlines": -2.0241, "s010_scare_quotes": 17.0, "s023_no_owner_identified": 0.0, "s072_loaded_category_tags": 9.0, "s076_conspiracy_tropes": 0.0696, "s078_absolutist_framing": 0.0, "s085_standard_sections": 2.0, "s097_suspicious_url": 0.0, "story_block_count": 24.0, "hero_image_area_frac": 0.01031, "thumbnail_size_uniformity": 0.7255, "s060_templated_imagery": 0.1902, "largest_face_area_frac": 0.00086, "small_face_count": 2.0, "s066_cluttered_blog_look": 0.0795, "inter_story_whitespace_frac": 0.2798, "content_column_count": "1", "side_margin_frac": 0.063, "header_band_height_frac": 0.032, "logo_centered": 1.0, "accent_hue": "red", "accent_color_count": 2.3804, "background_brightness": "white", "s067_alarmist_colors": 0.1027, "page_scroll_length": "medium", "nav_item_count": 69.0, "footer_link_count": 250.0, "cta_button_count": 4.0, "newsletter_box_count": 1.0, "social_icon_count": 0.0, "search_box_present": 1.0, "weather_widget_present": 0.0, "account_button_present": 1.0, "category_badge_count": 7.0, "mean_headline_word_count": 4.179, "headline_size_variance": 0.2213, "page_text_density": "dense", "timestamp_count": 3.0, "allcaps_token_frac": 0.0636, "number_token_frac": 0.012, "city_or_county_in_name": 1.0, "lx_ideology_identity_per100": 0.9749, "lx_attribution_per100": 0.0, "lx_timestamp_per100": 0.2089, "lx_loaded_outgroup_per100": 1.0446, "lx_health_scare_per100": 0.0, "lx_question_style_per100": 2.4373, "lx_culture_war_per100": 0.5571, "lx_market_money_per100": 0.2786, "lx_local_civic_per100": 0.2786}, "ocr_text": "MONTHLY REVIEW AN INDEPENDENT SOCIALIST MAGAZINE ano PRESS HOME ABOUT\u00bb MAGAZINE CONTACT \u2014 PRESS + Workers Suffer as Wealth Accumulates Artificial Intelligence, Energy Crisis & Ecological Crisis, The Destruction of Reason & the Rise of Ecofascism in the United States 1o3) NOTES FROM THE EDITORS etl persion June 2026 (Volume 78, Number 2) by The Editors June's \"Notes from the Editors\u201d revisits the fundamental concept of monopoly capitalism as expounded by Paul A. Baran and Paul M. Sweezy in their pathbreaking Monopoly Capital. The recent resurgence of the debate around Baran and Sweezy's work, the editors write, misses not only the enduring relevance of their analysis in an era of megacorporations and hyperscalers, but the revolutionary understanding of the economic system that arises from such an analysis. READ MORE Digital Imperialism and Emotional Tribute in East Asia by Jianlu Bi and Ting Zhou \"In the contemporary era of stagnant monopoly-finance capital,\" ianlu Bi and Ting Zhou begin, \u201cthe maintenance of global hegemony...ecesstates the systemic colonization of digital consciousness. In examining the presentation of Sino-Japanese political tension across three media outlets, the authors describe digitally manufactured hatreds that \u201cserve a vital imperialist function: the prevention of horizontal class solidarity\" that may threaten U.S hegemonic power in East Asa. + READ MORE ARTICLE Engels\u2019s Dialectics of Nature and Marxist Ecology by Chen Yiwen Yiwen Chen dives deep into Frederick Engels's Dialectics of Nature in order to give context to present-day debates surrounding the juxtaposition of the dialectics of nature and Marxist ecology. \u201cIt is hardly surprising that Engels's and Marx's ecological ideas are not entirely identical,\" Chen writes, \"Nevertheless, their ideas do possess an inherent consistency.\u201d READ MORE They are killing us off by Marge Piercy \u2018A new poem by Marge Piercy. 4+ READ MORE The Material Basis of a Spectre: Why China's Youth Are Rediscovering Mao by Yinhao Zhang. This article examines the resurgence of interest in Mao Zedong among China's youth, particularly a elite universities. This \"Mao fever\" reflects a complex mindset: indignation toward social inequality, uncertainty within a brutal job market, anda firm anti-imperialist and patriotic stance amidst the \"New Cold War.\" In Mao's legacy, a new generation is finding the guidance to navigate the sharpening contradictions of the present day. + READ MORE \u2014\u2014 HELP DONATE MR ONLINE The Thermodynamics of Capital: Artificial Intelligence, Energy Crisis, and Ecological Crisis by TeLi \u2018Te Li unravels the myth of digital dematerialization so heavily promulgated by Silicon Valley and other A boosters, which presents the technology as a phenomenon, that has escaped the material realm and thus, entropy itself. In fact, Li shows that the material and energetic requirements of AI places it squarely in the physical realm, then situates the technology within the context of the metabolic rift under capitalism, READ MORE The Destruction of Reason and the Rise of Ecofascism in the United States by Brett Clark and John Bellamy Foster MR associate editor Brett Clark and editor John Bellamy Foster explore \"the death drive of late imperialism\u201d and its manifestations in an ecological crisis that is not parallel to, but inseparable from, increasingly open ecofascism in the United States, Drawing from Luck\u00e9cs's Destruction of Reason and Istvin M\u00e9sziros's work on imperialism, Clark and Foster present a materialist analysis that illuminates the cult of unreason so pervasive under capitalism and imperialism while pointing to a path forward that is \u2018grounded in the historical-materialist Marxist tradition, + READ MORE Capitalism and the Crisis of Meaning in Our Times by Helena Sheehan Philosopher and frequent MR contributor Helena Sheehan considers the implications of living in a world in which a lack of meaning runs rampant, as well as how it is expressed in the literary world, If we lack coherent analysis, the varied crises of our times can seem disconnected and overpowering. A materially grounded Marxist worldview, she notes allows us to clear away the fog of meaninglessness engulfing contemporary society and chart path toward socialism, READ MORE a EO Gras Marx on the Circular Economy by Benjamin Selwyn \u2018Today, many use the term \"circular economy\" to describe a shift in the use of industrial waste products in a way that does not challenge the present mode of production. Returning to Marx, Benjamin Selwyn is able to show that this usage of the term is designed to facilitate the acquisitive demands of a capitalist economy, rather than a fundamental shift in resource use. READ MORE INE BRINK AND THE EIGHT REVOLUTIONS 5 THAT COULD LEAD TO SESS 01 NNI7ATION ANN CAFYISTENCE Vulnerable Israel by Tom Mayer In this review of Ilan Papp\u00e9's Israel on the Brink, Tom, \u2018Mayer considers the famed historian's assessment of the increasingly precarious Zionist state. In the book, Mayer writes, readers will find a thoughtful consideration of the past, present, and potential events that could shape a new vision for Palestine +R D MORE From Monthly Review CLASSICS ECOLOGY \u00e9a SEARCH Q. June 2026 (Volume 78, Number 2) MONTHLY REVIEW \u2014 nan Se civ hp Te Peel tar Ur gh Workers Suffer as Wealth Accumulates: Finance as Grift and Other Revelations by Fred Magdoff Fred Magdoff presents an incisive, data-driven analysis of the current state of the worker laboring under the domineering system of financialization and, in particular, private equity. In his conclusion, Magdoff asks: \"Is there a \u2018way out of twenty-first-century \u2018normal\u2019 for labor?\" \"The key way out,\u201d he answers, \u201cis an extraordinary growth of workers\u2019 power in order to combat the extraordinary power of capital\u2019\u2014one rooted in fully democratic socialist production and fundamental equality READ MORE REVIEW OF THE MONTH. The Fetishism of Al by John Bellamy Foster John Bellamy Foster takes on sweeping questions of artificial intelligence and its role in today\u2019s capitalist society. \"The Great Houses of Al are divided against themselves and cannot stand,\u201d he writes, \u201cIfhumanity is to flourish, the forces and relations of production must be revolutionized together...creating a world of sustainable human development.\u201d + READ MORE Slipping into Gogol's \u201cOvercoat\u201d: A Winter's Tale by Andy Merrifield Andy Merrifield reflects on Nikolai Gogol's short story \"The Overcoat,\u201d tying it to \"a distinctively Marxist problematic.\u201d In the protagonist ofthe tale, a Russian counsellor whose life is forever changed by the acquisition ofa new overcoat, Merrifield finds a character who, like Marx, knows firsthand what a single coat can tell us about the structures of society + READ MORE REVIEW OF THE MOI The Idea of the \u2018Uyghur Genocide\u2019 and the Realities of Xinjiang by Vijay Prashad and Tings Chak \u2018What is really happening in Xinjiang? Vijay Prashad and Tings Chak write: \"There is no evidence ofa policy of physical annihilation ofthe Uyghur peoples by the Chinese by the Israeli government against the occupied Palestinian systematic iling the hallmarks ofa genocide\u201d READ MORE SOURCE AmiLcar CABRAL Unequal Exchange Open Veins of Labor and. Albert Einstein's Marx's Ecology Discourse on Consciencism Return to the soot mene, Latin America Monopoly Capital \u201cWhy Socialism?\u201d Colonialism wane Numan Source Pare Berlei, tard Galeao, abel Harry Braverman paca John ime Cire stay Serequberhan, TepilLaseen, john Kiegge usar tbsl Harrys Bing Fone ames \u2018Hel Cota Tay Belamy Foner, Bret Scregecberkan Rose Gra ier In the public eye: Andy Merrifield\u2019s \u201cRoses for Gramsci\u201d The latest, in The Architec\u2019s Newspaper: \"There isa specter haunting the field of The latest: \"..1s'subaltern' a code for the \u2018working castes? Is hegemony\u2019 an economic force ora cultural power? Are \u2018organic intellectuals\u2019 inherently more progressive? READ MORE \u2018The answers to such questions depend upon your choice of scholar\u2014whether, say, you're reading a Foucauldian literary critic or a Marxist sociologist, a subaltern historian or a posthuman anthropologist. Over the years, Gramsci\u2019s writing has been polished by critics of such diverse persuasions that it has now become a mirror: One opens his books only to confirm one's own beliefs...when the English writer Andy Merrifield arrived in Rome, feeling \u2018washed out intellectually\u2019 Gramsci came to the rescue. + READ MORE In the public eye: C.G. Beck's \u201cThe Labor of Architecture\u201d architecture, and that specter i called \"Fuck the bosses\"\u2014or lass consciousness Stephen Maher. + READ MORE From Monthly Press Newsletter sign-up June 24: Workshop on lan Angus\u2019 \u201cMetabolic Rifts\u201d June 29th, join the Marxist Education Project for an online launch of Socialist Register 2026, featuring Catarina Principe together with Michael Roberts, Alfredo Saad-Filho, and June 27th: Online workshop on Socialist Register 2026 June 27th, join the Marxist Education Project for an online launch of Socialist Register 2026, featuring Catarina Principe together with Michael Roberts, Alfredo Saad-Filho, and Stephen Maher. + READ MORE Keep up to date with all the latest socialist news from MR.", "ocr_text_len": 9176}
