 investigated for halfa billion in suspicious transactions No owners, no agenda, no spin Just the facts This website usos cookies We use cookies to personalise content and ads, to provide social media features and to analyse our Yeon, trafic, We also share information about your use of our site with our social media, advertising and Allow solct Oh ATIVE analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you've provided to them or that they've \u2018ow seeeuon SOURNALISH \" collected trom your use of thie services Deny jebot locessay references ati jarkotin ow details \u2018We don\u2019t set the price, we take it\u2019: price squeeze leaves dairy farmers struggling to survive Lack of protections have left farmers vulnerable to a historic price swing that could put them out of business Published with INDIE 3%\u00ae FARMER See the online exhibition: Voices for Transparency A photography exhibition exploring how financial secrecy in British offshore jurisdictions has enabled corruption, wrongdoing and human rights abuses Behi human cost of financial secrecy \u2018Battery cattle\u2019 farms surge in UK as Big Dairy rakes in profit Nearly a fifth ofthe largest farms have been linked to pollution in recent years. Published with Gddfaian Protecting victims or \u2018validating hatred\u2019? MPs debate whether to publish the ethnicity of child sex offenders Some MPS argue the move would protect victims while others warning it would be used by those trying to sow division Local media needs to do better on its immigration reporting story published by Reach's regional outlets last week showcased the human cost of cheap clickbait | the story How we showcased the Aday at Tommy Robinson\u2019s rally: QR codes, crypto and an AI rapper Unite the Kingdom was billed as a people's protest. On the ground, it looked like an operation to turn grievance into clips, donations and sales Businessman admits to pocketing millions from Thurrock council Liam Kavanagh used taxpayers\u2019 cash to fund life of luxury and \u2018honestly believed he was entitled to the money\u2019 \u2018Green\u2019 UK pensions are bankrolling US fossil fuels Public sector pensions have ploughed billions into opaque investment funds which are financing ruinous gas projects on the US Gulf Coast Published with G Globatcapitat Beall \u2018Llost faith in the system\u2019: The teenager who took on the family courts and won Florence was taken from her mum aged 10. Then she embarked on a five- year battle to reverse court orders A-list libel lawyers cast clients as underdogs in bid to halt anti-SLAPP law London lawyers used a private government meeting to throw mud at the press and describe their super-rich clients as locked in a \u2018David and Goliath\u2019 battle \u2018No reasonable person can say it\u2019s OK\u2019: the baroness protecting children from chatbots Beeban Kidron has tabled a new law to keep kids safe from online harm. We spoke to her about getting tough on bigtech Illegal children\u2019s homes used as recruiting grounds for criminal gangs New report cites police, council and charity workers describing the acute dangers faced by children in unregistered settings Chelsea given record fine for payments revealed by Bureau investigation Premier League probe into offshore payments we revealed in 2023 concludes with \u00a310m fine and suspended transfer ban UK net migration is at its lowest in ten years - so why is the country fixated on Hostile political rhetoric and harmful media coverage overshadows statistics that show net migration is falling Fossil fuels aren\u2019t just ruining the planet, they\u2019re risking our pensions \u2018The conflict in Iran has shown up gas and oil as unsafe investments. It\u2019s time our pensions left them behind For years, bogus court experts have torn families apart. Has that finally come to an end? We've been reporting on unregulated experts because we hoped our scrutiny would help drive change. Now it has, After a string of failures, what next for the \u2018crackdown\u2019 on SLAPPs? Analysis: Until lawyers are barred from sending out empty threats on behalf of mega-rich clients, free speech remains in danger Inside the \u00a364bn pharma deal that could cost more lives than Covid With the NHS on its knees, the UK has struck a secretive new drugs deal. But how was it made ~ and what will it really cost? Karen Hao on AI: \u2018No one is asking whether this tech is actually helping people\u2019 We sat down with the Empire of AI author to discuss the global power dynamics shaping today\u2019s tech arms THE BUREAU OF \u00a9: INVESTIGATIVE JOURNALISM What's the true cost of the world\u2019s bestselling cancer drug? Pharma\u2019s biggest seller: how one drug makes more money than McDonald\u2019s We reveal how the bullish tactics of a pharma giant life-saving medicine have left taxpayers spending over the odds for its \u2018Major new project reveals the aggressive strategies used by MSD to make billions from a drug many desperate patients can't afford Hear about our latest investigations, before they break How we showcased the human cost of offshore secrecy Reporting on financial secrecy throws up aconstant challenge: who are the victims? So we set out to find them - and give their voices a platform \u2018The Bureau of Investigative Journalism The secret source who helped me cracka council scandal How a mysterious late-night email blew open the biggest story of my career Projects Got a Story? My mission to interview 100 Indigenous people ahead of Cop30 We've been investigating the threats faced by Indigenous people for years. But ahead of this year's climate summit, I wanted to do something different,", "ocr_text_len": 5616}
